-- What Cloud Computing Actually Is
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Explain cloud computing as renting someone else''s computers by the second, and name the five things that genuinely change when you do', 'Choose a region on the basis of latency, cost and where data is allowed to live, knowing which regions are nearest to Kenya', 'Say where the provider''s responsibility ends and yours begins, and why the cloud is not automatically cheaper'],
  notes = 'Cloud computing is renting computers you do not own, by the second, over the internet, and giving them back when you are done. Everything else is detail. The detail is where the money and the mistakes live.

THE FIVE THINGS THAT ACTUALLY CHANGE

1. YOU STOP BUYING AND START RENTING. A server used to be a thing you bought once and depreciated. Now it is a line on a monthly bill. That is good for starting and bad for forgetting - an idle server you bought is wasted capital, an idle server you rent keeps charging you every hour until you notice.

2. CAPACITY BECOMES A DIAL. You can go from one server to twenty in minutes for a traffic spike, then back. Nobody has to drive to a data centre. This is the single biggest genuine advantage.

3. YOU PAY FOR TIME AND TRAFFIC, NOT FOR WORK DONE. A server running all night doing nothing costs the same as one running all night serving customers. Storage costs by the gigabyte-month whether anybody reads it. Data leaving the provider''s network costs money per gigabyte, and this is the charge that surprises people most.

4. SOMEBODY ELSE OWNS THE HARDWARE. You never touch the machine. When it fails, it is replaced and your data on local disk is gone unless you planned for that.

5. THE DEFAULTS ARE NOT SAFE FOR YOU. A cloud account is powerful by default and private by default in some places and public by default in others. Knowing which is which is most of what the next four lessons teach.

THE THREE LAYERS, IN PLAIN TERMS

- INFRASTRUCTURE AS A SERVICE. You rent a bare virtual machine and you install and patch everything on it. Most control, most work. This is what EC2 is.
- PLATFORM AS A SERVICE. You hand over code and the provider runs it. Less control, far less maintenance.
- SOFTWARE AS A SERVICE. You just use the finished application. Gmail is this. You manage nothing but your data and your accounts.

Beginners often think learning the cloud means learning the first layer. In practice most small projects should start higher up, because the work you avoid is the work you will not do badly.

REGIONS AND AVAILABILITY ZONES

A REGION is a geographic cluster of data centres. An AVAILABILITY ZONE is one or more data centres inside a region, isolated so a failure in one does not take out the others. You choose a region when you create almost anything, and that choice has three consequences.

LATENCY. Distance costs milliseconds and milliseconds are felt. For users in Kenya:

- Cape Town is the only AWS region on the African continent, and the nearest by geography.
- The European regions, Ireland and Frankfurt among them, are heavily used from East Africa because undersea cable routes are good and the regions are cheap and complete.
- Middle East regions, Bahrain and the UAE, are also close.

Do not assume geographic closeness equals lowest latency. Routing follows cables, not maps. Test from where your users actually are before committing.

COST. The same virtual machine costs different amounts in different regions, sometimes by a wide margin. The African region is typically not the cheapest. If your users tolerate a little more latency, a cheaper region can halve a bill.

WHERE THE DATA LIVES. If you hold personal data about Kenyan residents, where that data is stored and whether it leaves the country is a legal question, not just a technical one. Kenya''s Data Protection Act 2019 regulates transferring personal data out of Kenya, with conditions. Confirm the current requirements with the Office of the Data Protection Commissioner before choosing a region for anything holding personal data; do not decide this from a tutorial.

THE SHARED RESPONSIBILITY LINE

This idea is examined on every cloud certification and matters more in real life.

The provider is responsible for the security OF the cloud: the buildings, the hardware, the hypervisor, the physical network.

You are responsible for security IN the cloud: who can log in, what your permissions allow, patching the operating system on a machine you rented, whether your storage is public, whether your database has a password, and whether your keys are in a public code repository.

Almost every cloud breach you read about is on your side of that line. The provider''s half very rarely fails.

THE CLOUD IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY CHEAPER

Say this plainly because the marketing does not. Renting is cheaper than owning when your usage varies or is small, and more expensive when your usage is large, steady and predictable. A permanently busy server often costs more per year in the cloud than the same box would have.

What the cloud reliably buys you is speed of starting, the ability to stop paying when you stop using, and not owning the failure of hardware. Those are real. "Cheaper" is conditional.

WHAT IT TAKES TO OPEN AN ACCOUNT FROM KENYA

Two practical realities to plan for:

- A valid payment card is required, and billing is in dollars. Some Kenyan debit cards are declined; a card enabled for international online payments is usually necessary. Expect a small verification charge.
- The FREE TIER is real but bounded, and it does not stop charging when you exceed it. There is no automatic cut-off. Exceeding a limit quietly produces a bill in dollars. The very first thing you will do in the next lesson is set a budget alert, before you create anything.',
  practice_activity = 'Make the abstract concrete before you create a single resource.

Step 1. Write down a real project you would actually build - a small booking site, a school results portal, an inventory tracker. One sentence.

Step 2. For that project, decide and write which of the three layers you would use, and why. If you wrote the infrastructure layer, write one more sentence justifying why you want to patch an operating system yourself.

Step 3. Test latency rather than guessing it. From your own connection, time a request to a public endpoint in two or three candidate regions - the African region, a European region and a Middle East region. Record the round-trip times. Note which was fastest and whether it matched what you expected from the map.

Step 4. Make a cost note. Look up the published hourly price of the same small instance type in three regions and write the three figures, plus the monthly cost of running it continuously. Multiply by the current shilling rate.

Step 5. Draw the responsibility line for your project. Make two columns, provider and you, and place at least eight items: physical security, operating system patches, who can log in, storage being public or private, database password, hardware failure, your access keys, network cabling.

Step 6. Answer one honest question in writing: for your project, which of the five changes in the notes is the reason you want the cloud at all? If the answer is only "it is cheaper", reread the section that says it is not.

Step 7. If your project will hold personal data about people in Kenya, write one line naming what you will confirm with the Office of the Data Protection Commissioner about storing or transferring that data before you choose a region.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "A storage bucket in your account is left publicly readable and customer records are downloaded. Under the shared responsibility model, whose failure is that?", "options": ["The provider''s, because they host the storage", "Yours, because access configuration is security IN the cloud and sits on your side of the line", "Shared equally between you and the provider", "Nobody''s, since public access is a supported feature"], "correct_index": 1}, {"question": "You run one busy server continuously all year with steady, predictable load. What is the honest cost comparison with owning the equivalent hardware?", "options": ["The cloud is always cheaper because you avoid capital expenditure", "The cloud is usually cheaper only when usage varies or is small; steady heavy load often costs more to rent", "Cost is identical by design", "Owning is always cheaper in every case"], "correct_index": 1}]'::jsonb
where id = '4bd7debe-7baf-4b5a-be15-a7f8a5a42d0e';

-- The AWS Management Console Tour
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Set a billing budget and free-tier alert before creating any resource, so a mistake costs an email instead of a bill', 'Lock down the root account with multi-factor authentication and work from a limited user instead', 'Use the region selector deliberately, and explain why resources appear to vanish when it changes'],
  notes = 'The console is a website that lets you create things that cost money. The order you learn it in matters, so this lesson is deliberately in the order a careful person would actually do it.

DO THESE THREE THINGS BEFORE CREATING ANYTHING

1. SET A BUDGET AND A FREE-TIER ALERT. In the billing section, create a budget with a small monthly figure - two or three dollars is enough to catch mistakes - and enable the free-tier usage alerts. The alert emails you when forecast or actual spend crosses the line.

Understand exactly what this does and does not do. It WARNS you. It does not STOP anything. There is no switch that caps your spending. A forgotten resource keeps charging after the alert arrives, which is why the alert has to arrive early and go to an address you read.

2. PUT MULTI-FACTOR AUTHENTICATION ON THE ROOT ACCOUNT. The root account is the email address you signed up with, and it can do absolutely everything including closing the account and changing the payment method. A stolen root password with no second factor is the worst thing that can happen to a cloud account. Add an authenticator app to it in the first ten minutes.

3. CREATE A NORMAL USER FOR YOURSELF AND STOP USING ROOT. Root is for a short list of account-level tasks and nothing else. Day-to-day work happens as a limited user with multi-factor authentication of its own. Lesson 5 covers how to scope that user properly.

Those three steps are the difference between a learning account and a horror story. Nothing else in this lesson is as important.

THE REGION SELECTOR, AND WHY YOUR WORK DISAPPEARS

Top right of the console is a region name. Almost everything you create belongs to one region and is invisible from every other region.

This produces the commonest beginner panic: you created a server yesterday, you log in today, the list is empty, and you assume it was deleted. It was not. The selector moved, or you logged in to a different default, and you are looking at a different region''s inventory - which charges you separately and shows you nothing of the other.

Three habits prevent it:

- Check the region before you create anything and before you conclude something is missing.
- Keep one project in one region unless you have a reason not to.
- Remember that a few services are GLOBAL rather than regional - identity management is one of them - so those look the same whichever region is selected.

HOW TO FIND THINGS AMONG TWO HUNDRED SERVICES

The service list is long enough to be useless to browse. Use the search box at the top and type what you want. Pin the handful you actually use to the navigation bar so you stop hunting.

For a beginner, the useful short list is small:

- A compute service for virtual machines
- An object storage service for files
- An identity service for users and permissions
- The billing and cost dashboard
- A monitoring service for alarms and logs

Everything else can wait until a real need appears. Clicking through unfamiliar services creating resources to see what they do is exactly how surprise bills happen.

THE BILLING DASHBOARD IS A PLACE YOU VISIT ON PURPOSE

Learn to read two views and visit them weekly:

- The cost breakdown BY SERVICE, which tells you what is charging you.
- The cost breakdown BY REGION, which is how you discover the instance you left running in a region you forgot about.

Look at the forecast as well as the current total. The forecast is what tells you a small daily charge is going to be a painful monthly one.

TAG EVERYTHING, FROM THE FIRST RESOURCE

A tag is a label you attach to a resource, such as project equals school-portal, or owner equals your name, or environment equals test. It costs nothing.

Do it from the start for one reason: in three months you will find a running resource and have no idea what it belongs to or whether deleting it will break something. An untagged account becomes a museum of things nobody dares switch off, and every one of them is on the bill.

THE HOUSEKEEPING RULE THAT SAVES THE MOST MONEY

Whenever you finish an experiment, delete what you created, in the same session, before you close the laptop. Not tomorrow.

And deleting is not always obvious: switching a virtual machine off still leaves its disk charging, a reserved public address costs money while unattached, and a storage bucket keeps charging for whatever is in it. The next two lessons name those specifically.

ONE MORE THING WORTH KNOWING EARLY

The console is only one way in. There is a command line tool and there are programming libraries, and professionals use those far more than the web pages because they are repeatable. You do not need them yet, but be aware that anything you click in the console is a call you could have scripted - and that the credentials those tools use are the access keys that lesson 5 warns you about at length.',
  practice_activity = 'Set up the account safely, in this order, and prove each step.

Step 1. Create the budget first. Set a monthly figure small enough to catch a mistake, enable free-tier alerts, and send them to an email address you actually read. Write down the figure you chose and the address.

Step 2. Enable multi-factor authentication on the root account using an authenticator app. Then sign out and sign back in to confirm it is actually required. Write down the date you did this.

Step 3. Write one sentence from memory on what the budget alert does and does not do. If your sentence suggests it stops spending, correct it.

Step 4. Note your region. Write the region name shown in the console, and the region name you intend to use for your project. If they differ, change the selector and say why you chose the one you did.

Step 5. Prove the region effect to yourself without spending anything. Open the virtual machine service, note the empty or populated list, switch regions, and look again. Write one line describing what changed, so the behaviour is familiar before it panics you.

Step 6. Pin five services to your navigation bar: compute, object storage, identity, billing, monitoring. Write the five names.

Step 7. Decide your tagging rule now, in one line: which tags you will put on every resource you ever create. For example project, owner and environment.

Step 8. Write your housekeeping rule as a sentence you will actually follow, naming the three things that keep charging after a server is switched off. If you cannot name all three yet, write the question down and answer it after the next two lessons.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "You set a monthly budget alert of three dollars and forget a running instance. What happens when spend crosses the budget?", "options": ["The instance is stopped automatically and charging ends", "You receive a notification, but nothing is stopped and charges continue", "The account is suspended until you pay", "The budget refunds the difference"], "correct_index": 1}, {"question": "You created a virtual machine yesterday and today the list is empty. What is the most likely explanation?", "options": ["The provider deleted it because it was idle", "The free tier expired and removed it", "The console is showing a different region, and almost all resources are region-scoped", "Your root account lost permission to see it"], "correct_index": 2}]'::jsonb
where id = '927fd35f-ec5c-4794-826d-9733b660bc5d';

-- EC2: Virtual Servers Explained
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Describe an instance as a rented computer billed while it runs, and name the charges that continue after you stop it', 'Launch and reach an instance safely: choose an image and size, handle the key pair, and write a security group that is not open to the world', 'Avoid the three mistakes that produce almost every surprise bill and almost every break-in'],
  notes = 'An instance is a computer you rent. It boots an operating system, you log in, and you install whatever you want. You are charged for the time it is RUNNING, usually by the second with a small minimum, at a rate set by its size and its region.

That sentence contains the whole mental model. The rest is the things it leaves out.

WHAT KEEPS CHARGING WHEN THE INSTANCE IS STOPPED

This is the first thing to learn, because it is the commonest unpleasant surprise.

- THE DISK. The virtual disk attached to your instance is a separate, independent thing. It is billed per gigabyte-month whether the instance is running, stopped, or has been stopped for six months. Stopping an instance does not free its disk.
- A RESERVED PUBLIC ADDRESS. A static public IP address costs nothing while attached to a running instance and costs money while it is sitting unused. The pricing is deliberately designed to discourage hoarding addresses, which means a stopped instance with a reserved address is charged for the address.
- SNAPSHOTS AND IMAGES. Any backup image you made is stored and billed until deleted.

So there are two different verbs and they mean different things. STOP keeps the machine and its disk, stops the compute charge, and lets you start it again with the same data. TERMINATE destroys the instance; by default it destroys the attached disk too, and that is permanent.

When you are finished with an experiment, terminate it and then check that the disk, any snapshot and any reserved address went with it.

THE FOUR CHOICES AT LAUNCH

AN IMAGE. The template the machine boots from: an operating system, sometimes with software preinstalled. Pick a current, official, long-term-support Linux image unless you have a reason not to. Unofficial images from a marketplace can carry their own hourly charge on top of the instance, which catches people out.

A SIZE. Instance types come in families aimed at different shapes of work - general purpose, compute-heavy, memory-heavy, storage-heavy - and within each family a range of sizes that roughly double in capacity and price as you go up. For learning, the smallest general-purpose type is right. Bigger is not better; it is only more expensive, and an oversized instance does not make a badly written application fast.

A KEY PAIR. This is how you prove who you are when you log in. The provider keeps the public half; you download the private half ONCE, at creation. Understand clearly: if you lose that private key file, you cannot log in to that instance again, and there is no reset button. Store it somewhere you will still have it in a year, keep its file permissions restrictive, and never commit it to a code repository.

A SECURITY GROUP. A firewall around the instance. By default it blocks all inbound traffic and allows all outbound. You open exactly the ports you need, from exactly the addresses that need them.

THE SECURITY GROUP MISTAKE THAT GETS PEOPLE BREACHED

The tutorial habit is to open the administrative login port to 0.0.0.0/0, meaning the entire internet, because it makes the tutorial work. Automated scanners find that port within minutes of it opening and begin trying credentials continuously. This is not a theoretical risk; it is the normal background condition of the internet.

Do this instead:

- Restrict the administrative port to your own address, or a small range you control. Your home address changes, so expect to update it; that minor inconvenience is the whole defence.
- Open the web ports to the world only when you are actually serving a website from that machine.
- Never open a database port to the world. A database belongs on a private network, reachable only from your application.
- Use key-based login and disable password login.

THE THREE MISTAKES THAT CAUSE MOST SURPRISE BILLS

1. LEAVING IT RUNNING. An instance left on for a month costs its hourly rate times roughly 730. A machine you meant to use for twenty minutes becomes a monthly subscription. The free tier offers a fixed number of hours a month on the smallest type for a limited period - and 750 hours is about one instance running continuously, so two instances exceed it even if each is small.

2. DATA TRANSFER OUT. Traffic from the provider to the internet is charged per gigabyte, and the first small amount each month is free. Serving video, large downloads or a popular site is where this becomes the biggest line on the bill, often larger than the compute itself. Traffic IN is generally free, which is why people never see it coming.

3. THE SECOND REGION. You create something in a region, switch regions, forget, and it runs for months. The cost dashboard broken down by region is how you find it. Check it.

WHAT AN INSTANCE IS NOT GOOD FOR

Be honest about this before building on it. A single instance is a single point of failure: the hardware under it can fail, and your data on its disk goes with it unless you planned backups. It needs patching, and patching is a job that does not do itself. For a small website or an internal tool it is fine and cheap. For anything that must not go down, you are looking at more than one instance, in more than one availability zone, which is a larger subject and a larger bill.

And for many small projects, a managed platform service that runs your code without you owning a machine is the better answer. Choosing an instance should be a decision, not a default.',
  practice_activity = 'Launch one instance, reach it safely, then destroy it completely and verify nothing is still charging.

Step 1. Before launching, write down: the region you are in, the image you will use, the instance type, and the hourly price of that type in that region. Multiply the hourly price by 730 and write the monthly figure in shillings.

Step 2. Create a key pair and download the private key. Store it somewhere deliberate and write down where. Set restrictive file permissions on it. Write one line stating what happens if you lose it.

Step 3. Write your security group rules before you create them:

- Administrative port: open to ____ (your own address, not the world)
- Web ports: open to ____ or not at all
- Anything else: ____

Step 4. Launch the instance with your tags attached - project, owner, environment - and connect to it with your key. Confirm you are logged in.

Step 5. Prove the firewall works. From the instance, note its public address. Then check that only the ports you opened are reachable, and that the administrative port is not reachable from an address you did not allow. Write what you observed.

Step 6. Now clean up properly, and this is the part that matters. Terminate the instance. Then go and check each of these separately and write down what you found:

- Is the attached disk gone, or still listed and charging?
- Did you reserve a public address? Is it released?
- Are there any snapshots or images left?

Step 7. Twenty-four hours later, open the cost dashboard broken down by service and by region. Write down every charge you see and what it is for. If something is still charging, find it and remove it. This step is the real lesson.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "You stop an instance you are not using. Which charges continue?", "options": ["None - stopping ends all charges for that instance", "Only the data transfer charge", "The attached disk continues to be billed per gigabyte-month, and a reserved public address is billed while unattached", "Only the operating system licence"], "correct_index": 2}, {"question": "A tutorial tells you to open the administrative login port to 0.0.0.0/0. What is the consequence?", "options": ["Nothing, since the port needs a key to be useful", "Automated scanners find it within minutes and begin continuous credential attempts, so it should be restricted to addresses you control", "The provider blocks the rule automatically", "It only matters for Windows instances"], "correct_index": 1}]'::jsonb
where id = 'ad76fd04-eb24-4c56-90cf-e08ef7b48ee0';

-- S3: Cloud Storage Explained
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Describe object storage and how it differs from a disk or a filesystem, and name the four things you are billed for', 'Keep a bucket private by default and share single files without making the bucket public', 'Choose a storage class knowing the retrieval cost and delay, and use versioning and lifecycle rules without quietly multiplying your bill'],
  notes = 'Object storage holds files - objects - inside containers called buckets, and gives each one an address. It is not a disk and not a filesystem, and the difference explains most of its behaviour.

HOW IT DIFFERS FROM A DISK

- You cannot mount it and edit a file in place. You PUT a whole object and you GET a whole object. Changing one line means uploading the object again.
- There are no real folders. A slash in a key name is just a character; the console draws folders to be kind to you.
- Durability is extremely high because every object is replicated across multiple facilities automatically. Losing an object to hardware failure is effectively not a thing you plan for.
- Capacity is unbounded from your side. You never provision a size.

That combination makes it right for images, documents, backups, logs, static website files and anything that is written once and read many times. It is wrong as a database and wrong as a working disk for an application.

BUCKET NAMES ARE GLOBALLY UNIQUE

A bucket name is unique across every customer of the provider worldwide, not just your account. That is why every obvious name is taken. Pick something with your project and organisation in it. Note also that a bucket name appears in URLs, so do not put anything confidential in the name itself.

THE FOUR THINGS YOU PAY FOR

Beginners expect one charge and find four:

1. STORAGE, per gigabyte-month, at a rate that depends on the storage class.
2. REQUESTS. Every upload, download and listing is a charged request, priced per thousand. Usually small, but an application that lists a bucket in a loop can make it large.
3. DATA TRANSFER OUT to the internet, per gigabyte. Same charge that bites on virtual machines, and for a popular file it dominates everything else.
4. RETRIEVAL, on the cold storage classes only, per gigabyte retrieved - and this is the one people do not read about until the bill arrives.

THE PUBLIC BUCKET CATASTROPHE

Historically, the worst and commonest cloud data leaks have been buckets accidentally made publicly readable. Customer records, identity documents, backups and internal files have all been found this way by people simply scanning for open buckets.

The provider now blocks public access by default at the account and bucket level. LEAVE THAT BLOCK ON. If a tutorial tells you to turn it off so your image loads, the tutorial is teaching you the thing that causes breaches.

To share files properly:

- FOR ONE FILE, TEMPORARILY: generate a presigned URL. It is a normal link with a signature and an expiry attached, so it works for whoever holds it until it expires, and the bucket stays private. This is the right answer for a download link, a receipt, an export.
- FOR A PUBLIC WEBSITE''S ASSETS: put a content delivery network in front of the bucket and let only the network read it. Users get the files, the bucket is never directly reachable, and you also get caching, which cuts your transfer bill.
- NEVER: make the whole bucket public because one file needed sharing.

Encrypt what you store. Server-side encryption is on by default now, and for anything sensitive consider managing the key yourself. Also turn on access logging before you need it, not after.

STORAGE CLASSES, AND THE RETRIEVAL TRAP

Classes trade storage price against access price and speed.

- STANDARD: most expensive to store, no retrieval fee, instant. Right for anything read regularly.
- INFREQUENT ACCESS: cheaper to store, a per-gigabyte retrieval fee, still instant. Right for things read a few times a year. Note the minimum storage duration - deleting early is charged as if you had kept it for the minimum.
- ARCHIVE AND DEEP ARCHIVE: very cheap to store, significant retrieval fee, and retrieval TAKES TIME - minutes to many hours depending on class and how much you are willing to pay to hurry it.

The trap is archiving something you will actually need in a hurry. An archive class is for records you must keep and expect never to read: closed-year accounts, old project files, compliance copies. It is the wrong place for last month''s backup that you might need on a bad Tuesday.

There is also an automatic tiering class that moves objects between tiers based on observed access, for a small monitoring fee per object. For a large bucket with unpredictable access it is often the sensible default; for a small bucket of many tiny objects the per-object fee can outweigh the saving.

VERSIONING AND LIFECYCLE RULES

VERSIONING keeps every version of an object, including the ones you delete. Switch it on and an accidental overwrite or deletion becomes recoverable, which is genuinely valuable.

The cost consequence is the part people miss: YOU ARE BILLED FOR EVERY VERSION. A file overwritten daily for a year is 365 stored objects. A bucket that looks nearly empty can hold a large and invisible bill in old versions. Deleting the visible object does not delete its versions.

LIFECYCLE RULES are how you manage that. They are rules on the bucket that act automatically: move objects to a cheaper class after N days, expire old versions after N days, delete incomplete multipart uploads after N days. That last one matters more than it sounds - a failed large upload leaves parts behind that are stored and billed and are invisible in the normal file listing.

Set three lifecycle rules on any bucket you intend to keep: expire noncurrent versions, abort incomplete uploads, and transition old objects to a cheaper class if that fits your access pattern.

IS THIS A BACKUP?

Not by itself. A copy in the same account, reachable by the same credentials, is not protected against the thing most likely to destroy your data - a mistake or a compromised key deleting both. A real backup needs versioning on, and ideally a copy in a separate account or with deletion protection, so that one bad credential cannot remove everything.',
  practice_activity = 'Build a private bucket, share a file from it without making it public, and set the rules that stop it costing more than you think.

Step 1. Create a bucket in your chosen region. Write down the name and confirm that public access block is ON at both account and bucket level.

Step 2. Upload one small file. Then try to open it in a browser using its plain URL. It should fail. Write down what error you got - this is what correct configuration looks like.

Step 3. Now share that same file properly. Generate a presigned URL with a short expiry, open it, and confirm it works. Wait for it to expire and confirm it stops working. Write both results.

Step 4. Turn on versioning. Upload a different file with the same name three times. Then look at the versions view and count the objects actually stored. Write the number, and write one sentence about what that means for a file your application overwrites every hour.

Step 5. Add three lifecycle rules and write down the days you chose for each:

- Expire noncurrent versions after ____ days
- Abort incomplete multipart uploads after ____ days
- Transition objects to a cheaper class after ____ days, or state why you did not

Step 6. Price a real scenario in shillings. Assume 20 GB stored and 100 GB downloaded by users in a month. Look up the storage price per gigabyte-month and the transfer-out price per gigabyte in your region, and calculate both lines. Write which is bigger. For most small projects the answer surprises people.

Step 7. Decide the class for two real things: a file your users download weekly, and your 2024 accounting records. Write the class for each and, for the archive one, write the retrieval time and fee you would face if you needed it urgently.

Step 8. Delete the test objects, their versions, and the bucket. Then check the cost dashboard a day later and confirm nothing remains.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "One image in your bucket needs to be downloadable by a customer. What is the right way to do it?", "options": ["Turn off public access block and make the bucket public", "Generate a presigned URL with an expiry, leaving the bucket private", "Email the private key file to the customer", "Move the object to an archive class first"], "correct_index": 1}, {"question": "You enable versioning and your application overwrites the same 5 MB file every hour for a month. What is the storage effect?", "options": ["Still 5 MB, because only the current version is stored", "About 10 MB, since one previous version is kept", "Roughly 720 stored versions billed as separate objects, which is why a lifecycle rule should expire noncurrent versions", "Nothing is billed until you restore a version"], "correct_index": 2}]'::jsonb
where id = 'fd4ed5d2-0308-4d97-bf3d-660a2fcfea15';

-- IAM: Controlling Who Can Do What
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Distinguish users, groups, roles and policies, and explain why a role is safer than a key for anything running inside the cloud', 'Apply least privilege in practice, and say why administrator access on your daily account is how accounts get drained', 'Recognise a leaked access key as the single commonest cause of catastrophic cloud bills, and run the right response if it happens'],
  notes = 'Identity and access management answers one question: who is allowed to do what. It is the service that decides whether every single other call succeeds, which makes it the most important one to understand and the easiest to configure badly.

It is also GLOBAL, not regional. Users and policies look the same whichever region the console is showing.

THE FOUR NOUNS

A USER is a person or an application with its own long-lived credentials. A user can have a console password and can have access keys.

A GROUP is a bundle of users that shares permissions. Put permissions on groups, put people in groups. Permissions attached directly to individuals become impossible to audit within a month.

A POLICY is a document listing allowed or denied actions on specified resources. Policies are the permissions. Nothing is allowed unless a policy allows it, and an explicit deny always wins over any allow.

A ROLE is a set of permissions with NO permanent credentials, which something assumes temporarily and receives short-lived credentials for. This is the important one, and the next section is why.

WHY ROLES ARE THE ANSWER FOR ANYTHING RUNNING IN THE CLOUD

Suppose your application on a virtual machine needs to read a storage bucket.

The naive approach is to create a user, generate access keys, and put those keys in a configuration file on the machine. That works, and it is the pattern behind an enormous share of cloud breaches. The keys do not expire. They sit in a file. They get copied into a code repository, into a container image, into a screenshot, into a support ticket.

The correct approach is to attach a ROLE to the instance. The application then obtains temporary credentials automatically, they rotate on their own, they expire, and there is no key anywhere on disk to leak.

Rule to carry with you: if the thing needing access is running inside the cloud, it gets a role. Access keys are for the comparatively rare case of something outside that genuinely cannot use a role, and even then there are better modern options worth looking for first.

LEAST PRIVILEGE, IN PRACTICE RATHER THAN IN PRINCIPLE

Least privilege means granting exactly the permissions needed for the job and nothing more. Everybody agrees with it and almost nobody starts that way, because the broad built-in administrator policy makes everything work immediately.

Here is why that matters more in the cloud than in most places. A compromised credential with administrator rights can do everything the account can do, including launching very expensive compute in every region at once. That is not a hypothetical; it is the standard pattern after a key leak, because compute is what attackers monetise. A limited credential confines the damage to what it was allowed to touch.

A workable discipline:

- Start from the built-in job-function policies rather than full administrator, and narrow from there.
- When something fails, read the error - it names the action and resource that was denied - and add that one permission rather than widening to everything.
- Scope policies to specific resources, not to the wildcard, wherever you can.
- Review and remove permissions you granted temporarily. Nobody ever does this unless it is on a list, so put it on a list.
- Use the access-analysis tooling the service provides to see which granted permissions have never been used, and remove them.

ACCESS KEYS: THE SINGLE BIGGEST PRACTICAL RISK

Access keys are a key id and a secret. The secret is shown once. They never expire by themselves. They are the credential that leaks.

The usual route is a public code repository. Somebody commits a configuration file, or a notebook, or a test script, and the key is in it. Automated scanners watch public repositories continuously and find new keys within minutes. The usual outcome is large-scale compute launched for cryptocurrency mining across many regions, producing a bill in the thousands of dollars within a day or two.

Note the detail that catches people: removing the key in a later commit does not help, because the old commit is still in the repository history. The key must be deactivated, not edited out.

So:

- Never put a key in code, in a repository, in a public notebook, in a chat message, or in a screenshot.
- Use a role instead whenever the thing is inside the cloud.
- Keep keys out of files that get committed. Use environment variables or a secrets manager, and add the files holding them to your ignore list.
- Turn on a repository secret-scanning feature if you have one.
- Rotate keys on a schedule and delete the ones nobody uses. The service reports when each key was last used; anything unused for months should go.
- Put multi-factor authentication on every user with console access, not only on root.

IF A KEY LEAKS, DO THIS IN THIS ORDER

Speed matters more than tidiness. Minutes count.

1. DEACTIVATE OR DELETE THE KEY immediately in the identity console. Do not try to work out the blast radius first.
2. Create a replacement key for the legitimate workload and get it running again.
3. Look at the audit trail - the service that records every API call - for activity you did not perform, and check every region, not just yours.
4. Terminate anything you did not create. Attackers routinely launch in regions nobody uses.
5. Check for identities you did not make: new users, new keys on existing users, new roles with trust policies pointing somewhere unfamiliar. A competent attacker creates a second way in.
6. Contact the provider''s support. Unauthorised-use bills caused by a leaked key are often reduced or waived, but you have to ask, and promptly, and be able to show what you did about it.
7. Then fix the cause: the role you should have used, the file you should not have committed.

THE ROOT ACCOUNT, ONE MORE TIME

Root can do everything, including things no policy can restrict. It should have multi-factor authentication, no access keys at all, a long unique password, and should be used only for the short list of tasks that genuinely require it. If your root account has access keys, delete them today; there is almost no legitimate reason for them to exist.',
  practice_activity = 'Build the permission setup you should have had from day one, then rehearse the leak response.

Step 1. Audit what you already have. Write down: does root have multi-factor authentication, does root have any access keys, how many users exist, and which of them have administrator permissions. Fix the first two now if either is wrong.

Step 2. Create a group with a narrow, job-focused policy - read-only on object storage, for example - and a user inside it. Give that user multi-factor authentication and a console password.

Step 3. Test least privilege by hitting the wall deliberately. As that user, try to do something the policy does not allow. Read the error and write down exactly which action and which resource it names. Then add only that one permission and try again. This loop is the actual skill.

Step 4. Replace a key with a role. Create a role that permits reading one specific bucket, attach it to an instance, and confirm from inside the instance that it can read the bucket with no key stored anywhere. Then write one sentence on why this is safer than a key in a configuration file.

Step 5. Review your keys. For every access key in the account, write the key id, what uses it, and when it was last used. Delete every one you cannot justify in a sentence.

Step 6. Write your leak-response plan on one page, in order, from the notes: deactivate, replace, read the audit trail across all regions, terminate what you did not create, hunt for identities you did not create, contact support, fix the cause. Keep it where you would find it in a panic.

Step 7. Prevent the commonest leak. Check that your project ignores the files that hold credentials, and confirm no key is in your repository history. Write down how you checked.

Step 8. Write the one-line rule you will follow from now on: anything running inside the cloud gets a ____, not a ____.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Your application on a virtual machine needs to read a storage bucket. What is the right way to give it access?", "options": ["Create a user, generate access keys and put them in a configuration file on the machine", "Attach a role to the instance so it receives short-lived credentials automatically, with no key stored on disk", "Use the root account''s access keys, since they always work", "Make the bucket public so no credentials are needed"], "correct_index": 1}, {"question": "You discover an access key of yours was committed to a public repository an hour ago. What is the correct first action?", "options": ["Remove the key from the file and push a new commit", "Work out what the key could access before changing anything", "Deactivate or delete that key immediately, then investigate the audit trail across all regions", "Wait to see whether anything is actually charged"], "correct_index": 2}]'::jsonb
where id = '0e300d5e-a3e8-4618-aa79-a8f9f3043d04';

-- The AWS Cloud Practitioner Certification Path
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['State what the foundational certification tests, what it costs in shillings, and what it does and does not do for your employability', 'Build a realistic study plan from free official material, and know the exam format and pass mark before booking', 'Choose between an online-proctored and a test-centre sitting with the Kenyan practicalities of each in mind'],
  notes = 'The foundational cloud certification is an entry-level, mostly non-technical exam about concepts, economics, security responsibilities and the main services. It is a reasonable first step and it is routinely oversold. Both halves of that sentence matter.

Exam details, fees and policies change. Verify every figure below against the official certification pages and the current exam guide before you book anything.

WHAT IT ACTUALLY TESTS

Expect four areas, weighted roughly like this:

- CLOUD CONCEPTS. What the cloud is, the value proposition, the economics, the design principles.
- SECURITY AND COMPLIANCE. The shared responsibility model above all, identity and access concepts, where to find compliance documentation.
- TECHNOLOGY AND SERVICES. What the main compute, storage, database, networking and monitoring services are FOR. Not how to configure them in depth.
- BILLING, PRICING AND SUPPORT. Pricing models, the cost tools, budgets, the support plans.

Note the shape of that. It is a vocabulary and judgement exam, not a hands-on exam. You are asked which service fits a described need, not to write a policy document.

FORMAT, AND THE NUMBERS TO CONFIRM

- Around 65 questions, multiple choice and multiple response, in roughly 90 minutes.
- Scored on a scaled range with a pass mark stated in the exam guide, and some questions are unscored research items that do not count.
- The fee is around 100 United States dollars. Convert that honestly: at a shilling rate in the low 130s that is well over 13,000 shillings, before any study material. For most Kenyan learners this is a real decision, not a formality, and it is a reason to be ready before booking rather than booking to create pressure.
- Look for discount vouchers. The provider periodically offers partial-discount vouchers for completing official free training or an official practice exam, and there are also free digital badge programmes that occasionally come with voucher codes. Checking takes ten minutes and can save a meaningful share of the fee.

A STUDY PLAN THAT DOES NOT COST ANYTHING

The free official material is genuinely sufficient for this exam.

- THE EXAM GUIDE. Read it first, not last. It lists the domains, their weightings and the in-scope services. It is the specification; everything else is commentary.
- THE PROVIDER''S OWN FREE TRAINING. There is a free digital training path aimed at exactly this certification. Use it as the backbone.
- THE OFFICIAL SAMPLE AND PRACTICE QUESTIONS. Use these to calibrate, not to learn. If you are reliably scoring comfortably above the pass mark on official practice material, you are ready.
- A SMALL NUMBER OF WHITEPAPERS. The overview, the well-architected framework, and the pricing and cost material. Do not try to read the whole library.
- YOUR OWN ACCOUNT. The four previous lessons in this course are worth more to this exam than any video, because concepts you have actually done are the ones you recall under time pressure.

A realistic timeline for somebody studying part-time around work or school is three to six weeks. Less if you already work with infrastructure, more if the whole vocabulary is new. The commonest failure is not lack of study but memorising service names without understanding which problem each one solves, because the questions are scenario-shaped.

SITTING THE EXAM FROM KENYA

Two routes, and the practicalities differ.

A TEST CENTRE. Authorised testing centres operate in Nairobi and in some other major towns. You go, you sit in a controlled room, their equipment and connection are the ones being used. For most people this is the lower-risk option, and the one to choose if your home connection or power is unreliable.

ONLINE PROCTORED, FROM HOME. Convenient, and genuinely demanding of your environment. Expect the requirements to include: a private room with a closed door, nobody else entering for the duration, a clear desk, a working webcam and microphone, a continuous and stable internet connection, and a scan of the room before you start. Power is the risk nobody plans for - an outage mid-exam is a disrupted session you may have to escalate to reschedule.

Decide honestly which describes your situation. An exam lost to a power cut costs the whole fee again.

WHAT THE CERTIFICATE DOES AND DOES NOT DO

Be clear-eyed, because this is where people are misled.

IT DOES: give you a shared vocabulary with cloud teams, prove you were willing to study something to a standard, get you past some automated screening filters, and prepare the ground for the associate-level certifications that employers actually weight.

IT DOES NOT: qualify you for a cloud engineering job on its own. It is foundational and widely held. Hiring managers for technical cloud roles generally treat it as a starting signal, not as evidence you can build and operate anything.

What converts it into a job is EVIDENCE. Something you deployed that is running and can be looked at. A small project with its architecture written down - what you built, which services, what it costs a month, what you would change at ten times the traffic. One such project discussed confidently outweighs the certificate in an interview, and the certificate plus the project is a genuinely strong entry-level position.

WHERE TO GO AFTERWARDS

- If you want to build and design systems: the associate-level solutions architect certification. Substantially harder, substantially more respected.
- If you want to operate systems: the associate-level systems operations route.
- If you want to write software on the platform: the associate-level developer route.
- If cloud security interests you: deepen identity and access first, since it underpins every other security topic.

Certifications expire, typically after three years, and recertifying means sitting a current exam. Factor that into whether and when the spend is worth it.

ONE LAST PRACTICAL POINT

Study inside the free tier with a budget alert already set, as lesson 2 insisted. Running a small amount of real infrastructure while you study is the cheapest possible way to make the material stick - and the student who has actually terminated an instance and then found the orphaned disk still charging understands the billing domain better than anybody who only read about it.',
  practice_activity = 'Decide whether to sit this exam on evidence, then build a plan with real dates and a real cost.

Step 1. Open the official certification page and the current exam guide. Write down, from the pages themselves rather than from this lesson: the exam code, the number of questions, the time allowed, the pass mark, the fee in dollars, and the validity period.

Step 2. Convert the fee to shillings at today''s rate and write the figure. Then write one sentence on whether that is a reasonable spend for you now, and what you expect in return.

Step 3. Spend ten minutes checking for a discount voucher - official free training that grants one, or a digital badge programme. Write what you found.

Step 4. Take the official practice questions cold, before studying. Write your score. This tells you how far you actually have to go and prevents both over-studying and booking too early.

Step 5. Build a four-week plan with dates. Week by week, name the domain you will cover and the free official resource you will use. Add one hands-on task per week drawn from lessons 2 to 5 of this course - set a budget, launch and fully clean up an instance, make a presigned URL, attach a role instead of a key.

Step 6. Choose your sitting. Write down: test centre or online proctored, and the reason. If you choose online, list the environment requirements you must meet and write your plan for a power outage mid-exam. If you cannot answer that, choose a test centre.

Step 7. Build the thing that matters more than the certificate. Write a one-page description of a small project you will deploy: what it does, which services, the estimated monthly cost in shillings, and one sentence on what you would change if traffic grew tenfold. Deploy it, then clean it up and keep the page.

Step 8. Set your booking rule now: you will book the exam only once you are scoring comfortably above the pass mark on official practice material. Write the score you will require.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What does the foundational cloud certification mainly test?", "options": ["Hands-on configuration of networking and policies under time pressure", "Concepts, security responsibilities, which service fits a described need, and billing and pricing models", "Writing infrastructure code from memory", "Only the shared responsibility model"], "correct_index": 1}, {"question": "You pass the foundational exam and apply for cloud roles with nothing else. What should you honestly expect?", "options": ["It qualifies you for a cloud engineering role on its own", "It is a starting signal that gets you past some filters, but evidence of something you actually deployed is what converts it into interviews", "Employers generally value it above the associate-level certifications", "It replaces the need for any practical experience"], "correct_index": 1}]'::jsonb
where id = 'f759d57b-4405-429d-bc37-6d5b0a8dc1bd';
